.SH NAME
sccsfile \- format of SCCS file
.SH DESCRIPTION
An SCCS file is an ASCII file.
It consists of six logical parts:
the
.IR checksum ,
the
.I "delta table"\^
(information about each delta),
.I "user names"\^
(login names and/or numerical group IDs of users who add deltas),
.I flags\^
(definitions of internal keywords),
.I comments\^
(arbitrary descriptive information about the file),
and the
.I body\^
(the actual text lines intermixed with control lines).
.PP
Throughout an SCCS file there are lines beginning with the
\fBASCII SOH\fR (start of heading) character (octal 001).
This character is hereafter referred to as
.I "the control character"\^
and is represented graphically as \fB@\fR.
Any line described below which is not depicted as beginning with
the control character is prevented from beginning
with the control character.
.PP
Entries of the form DDDDD represent a five digit string
(a number between 00000 and 99999).
.PP
Each logical part of an SCCS file is described in detail below.
.SH Checksum
The first line of an SCCS file.
The form of the line is:
.if !\ns .sp
.ti +5
@\fBhDDDDD\fR
.br
.sp
The value of the checksum is the sum of all characters, except
those of the first line.
The @\fBh\fR provides a
.I "magic number"\^
of (octal) 064001.
.SH Delta table
Consists of a variable number of entries of the form:
.if !\ns .in +5
.if \ns .sp
.if \ns .ps -1
.nf
@\fBs\fR DDDDD/DDDDD/DDDDD
.if t @\fBd\fR <type> <\fBSCCS ID\fR>  yr/mo/da hr:mi:se  <pgmr>  DDDDD  DDDDD
.if n @d <type> <\fBSCCS ID\fR> yr/mo/da hr:mi:se <pgmr> DDDDD DDDDD
@\fBi\fR DDDDD \fB...\fR
@\fBx\fR DDDDD \fB...\fR
@\fBg\fR DDDDD \fB...\fR
@\fBm\fR <MR number>
  \fB.\fR
  \fB.\fR
  \fB.\fR
@\fBc\fR <comments> \fB...\fR
  \fB.\fR
  \fB.\fR
  \fB.\fR
@\fBe\fR
.fi
.if !\ns .in -5
.if \ns .ps +1
.sp
The first line (@\fBs\fR) contains the number of lines
inserted/deleted/unchanged respectively.
The second line (@\fBd\fR) contains the type of the delta
(currently, normal: \fBD\fR, and removed: \fBR\fR), the SCCS
ID of the delta, the date and time of creation of the delta,
the login name corresponding to the real user ID at the time
the delta was created, and the serial numbers of the delta
and its predecessor, respectively.
.sp
The @\fBi\fR, @\fBx\fR, and @\fBg\fR lines contain the serial
numbers of deltas included, excluded, and ignored, respectively.
These lines are optional.
.sp
The @\fBm\fR lines (optional) each contain one MR number associated
with the delta;
the @\fBc\fR lines contain comments associated with the delta.
.sp
The @\fBe\fR line ends the delta table entry.
.SH User names
The list of login names and/or numerical group IDs of users who
may add deltas to the file, separated by new-lines.
The lines containing these login names and/or numerical group
IDs are surrounded by the bracketing lines @\fBu\fR and @\fBU\fR.
An empty list allows anyone to make a delta.
.SH Flags
Keywords used internally (see
.BR admin (1)
for more information on their use).
Each flag line takes the form:
.sp
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR <flag>	<optional text>
.br
.sp
The following flags are defined:
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR t	<type of program>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR v	<program name>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR i
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR b
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR m	<module name>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR f	<floor>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR c	<ceiling>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR d	<default-sid>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR n
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR j
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR l	<lock-releases>
.ti +5
@\fBf\fR q	<user defined>
.br
.sp
.TP
.B b
The
.B \-b
keyletter can be used on the
.I get\^
command to cause a branch in the delta tree.
.TP
.B c
Defines the "ceiling" release;
the release above which no deltas may be added.
.TP
.B d
Defines the default SID to be used when none is specified on a
.B get\^
command.
.TP
.B f
Defines the "floor" release;
the release below which no deltas may be added.
.TP
.B i
Controls the warning/error aspect of the "No id keywords" message.
When the
.B i
flag is not present, this message is only a warning;
when the
.B i
flag is present, this message will cause a "fatal" error
(the file will not be gotten, or the delta will not be made).
.TP
.B j
Causes
.B get\^
to allow concurrent edits of the same base SID.
.TP
.B l
Defines a
.I list\^
of releases that are
.I locked\^
against editing (\c
.BR get (1)
with the
.B \-e
keyletter).
.TP
.B m
Defines the first choice for the replacement text of
the \fB%M%\fR identification keyword.
.TP
.B n
Causes
.B delta\^
to insert a "null" delta (a delta that applies
.I no\^
changes) in those releases that are skipped when a delta is made in a
.I new\^
release (e.g., when delta 5.1 is made after delta 2.7, releases 3
and 4 are skipped).
The absence of the
.B n
flag causes skipped releases to be completely empty.
.TP
.B q
Defines the replacement for the \fB%Q%\fR identification keyword.
.TP
.B t
Defines the replacement for the \fB%Y%\fR identification keyword.
.TP
.B v
Controls prompting for MR numbers in addition to comments;
if the optional text is present it defines an MR number validity
checking program.
.SH Comments
Arbitrary text surrounded by the bracketing lines @\fBt\fR and @\fBT\fR.
The comments section typically will contain a description of the
file's purpose.
.SH Body
The body consists of text lines and control lines.
Text lines don't begin with the control character,
control lines do.
There are three kinds of control lines:
\fIinsert\fR, \dIdelete\fR, and \fIend\fR, represented by:
.sp
.ti +5
@\fBI\fR DDDDD
.ti +5
@\fBD\fR DDDDD
.ti +5
@\fBE\fR DDDDD
.br
.sp
respectively.
The digit string is the serial number corresponding to the
delta for the control line.
.SH SEE ALSO
admin(1),
delta(1),
get(1),
prs(1).
.sp
.I "Source Code Control System User's Guide"\^
in the ZEUS Utilities Manual
